---
import ArticleLayout from '../../layouts/ArticleLayout.astro';
import Callout from '../../components/Callout.astro';

const baseUrl = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const join = (path: string) => `${baseUrl.replace(/\/$/, '')}${path}`;
---

<ArticleLayout
  title="auth0-sec"
  description="Inspect a subject — IP, user, or tenant policy — to surface block state, lockouts, and attack-protection configuration."
  eyebrow="Tutorial"
  lede="After this page you will be able to check whether an IP is blocked, whether a user is locked out, and what the LINQ sandbox tenant's brute-force and breached-password policies look like — all from a single command."
  breadcrumb={[
    { label: 'Home', href: join('/') },
    { label: 'Tutorials', href: join('/tutorials/') },
    { label: 'auth0-sec' },
  ]}
>
  <Callout variant="info" label="TL;DR">
    <ul>
      <li><strong>What it does:</strong> Classifies your subject (IP, email, user ID, or the word "policy") and queries the relevant Auth0 anomaly-detection and attack-protection endpoints.</li>
      <li><strong>When to reach for it:</strong> Any time you need to answer "is this IP blocked?", "is this user locked out?", or "what is our brute-force policy?"</li>
      <li><strong>The one command to know:</strong> <code>/auth0-sec 1.2.3.4</code></li>
    </ul>
  </Callout>

  <h2 id="when-to-use">When to use this skill</h2>
  <p>Use <code>auth0-sec</code> when a LINQ engineer or support analyst needs to understand the security posture of the LINQ sandbox tenant at a point in time. The skill responds to phrasing such as "is this IP blocked," "is user X locked out," "what's our brute-force policy," "breached-password policy," "suspicious IP throttling," or "auth0 security posture." It is read-only across all five endpoints it touches, so it is safe to run during an incident without risk of changing state.</p>
  <p>This skill is <em>not</em> for per-user investigations beyond block status (use <code>ha-debug get-user</code> or <code>/auth0-logs</code>), specific event lookup (use <code>/auth0-logs</code>), aggregate dashboard views (use <code>/auth0-stats</code>), configuration changes, destructive operations such as unblocking an IP, or real-time alerting. All of those routes lie outside this skill's read-only scope.</p>

  <h2 id="prerequisites">Prerequisites</h2>
  <Callout variant="warn" label="Missing scopes">
    If the script exits with <code>auth_failed</code> and the hint mentions "scope," the M2M app is missing one or more of the required scopes below. See <code>docs/developer/onboarding.md</code> for the upgrade path.
  </Callout>
  <ul>
    <li><strong><code>AUTH0_DOMAIN</code></strong>, <strong><code>AUTH0_CLIENT_ID</code></strong>, <strong><code>AUTH0_CLIENT_SECRET</code></strong> — set in <code>.env</code> at the repo root.</li>
    <li><strong>Required M2M scopes:</strong> <code>read:anomaly_blocks</code>, <code>read:attack_protection</code>, and <code>read:users</code> on the LINQ sandbox tenant.</li>
    <li><strong>First-time setup:</strong> <code>cp .env.example .env</code>, fill in credentials, then verify scopes are granted in the Auth0 Dashboard. See <code>docs/developer/onboarding.md</code>.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="walkthrough">Walkthrough</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>
      <p><strong>Invoke — IP check:</strong></p>
      <pre><code>/auth0-sec 1.2.3.4</code></pre>
      <p>The skill classifies the subject as an IP address, queries <code>{'/anomaly/blocks/ips/{ip}'}</code> for block state, and pulls recent log activity for that IP to give context alongside the block verdict.</p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p><strong>Invoke — policy check:</strong></p>
      <pre><code>/auth0-sec policy</code></pre>
      <p>The keyword <code>policy</code> triggers queries against all three attack-protection endpoints: breached-password, brute-force, and suspicious-IP throttling. No pre-flight is shown — the skill is read-only and the queries are narrow.</p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p><strong>Read the response.</strong> For an IP, focus on the <code>block.blocked</code> boolean, the event count from <code>recent_activity</code>, and the <code>top_users</code> list. For a policy check, focus on the <code>enabled</code> flag, the <code>max_attempts</code> threshold, and the action on detection for each policy.</p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p><strong>Common follow-ups.</strong> If the IP is not blocked but shows recent failures, run <code>/auth0-stats failures</code> to see whether this IP is part of a wider pattern, or move to <a href={join('/tutorials/ha-debug/')}>ha-debug</a> to inspect a specific affected user's full state.</p>
    </li>
  </ol>

  <Callout variant="self-check" label="Did it work?">
    <ul>
      <li>The response leads with "Security check for IP …" or "Block check for user …" or "Tenant-wide Auth0 security posture"—not raw JSON.</li>
      <li>For an IP subject, you see a "blocked" or "not currently blocked" verdict within the first bullet of the summary.</li>
      <li>For a policy subject, you see one bullet per policy (breached-password, brute-force, suspicious-IP throttling) with the enabled state called out.</li>
    </ul>
  </Callout>

  <h2 id="output-shape">Output shape</h2>
  <p>The script writes a JSON envelope to stdout whose shape varies by subject kind. For an IP subject the envelope contains <code>subject</code>, <code>subject_kind</code>, a <code>block</code> object with a <code>blocked</code> boolean, and a <code>recent_activity</code> object. See <a href="https://github.com/shannoncarver/hackathon-may-2026/blob/main/.claude/skills/auth0-sec/SKILL.md"><code>.claude/skills/auth0-sec/SKILL.md</code></a> for the full schema.</p>
  <pre><code>{`{
  "subject": "1.2.3.4",
  "subject_kind": "ip",
  "block": { "blocked": true },
  "recent_activity": {
    "days": 7,
    "total": 9,
    "breakdown": { "fp": 7, "fu": 2 },
    "top_users": [{ "user_name": "alice@example.com", "count": 5 }]
  }
}`}</code></pre>

  <h2 id="troubleshooting">Troubleshooting</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong><code>missing_env</code>:</strong> <code>.env</code> is not populated. Run <code>cp .env.example .env</code> and fill in the Auth0 credentials. See <code>docs/developer/onboarding.md</code>.</li>
    <li><strong><code>auth_failed</code>:</strong> The M2M token request failed. Read the <code>hint</code> field from the stderr JSON — if it mentions "scope," the M2M app is missing <code>read:anomaly_blocks</code>, <code>read:attack_protection</code>, or <code>read:users</code>; if it mentions the cache, delete <code>.auth0-token.json</code> and retry.</li>
    <li><strong><code>bad_subject</code> (exit 8):</strong> Subject classification failed. The skill supports IPv4, IPv6, email addresses (<code>@</code>-prefixed), IdP-prefixed user IDs (<code>auth0|…</code>), and the keywords <code>policy</code>, <code>status</code>, and <code>posture</code>.</li>
    <li><strong><code>rate_limited</code>:</strong> Auth0 sandbox rate limits apply. Wait a moment and retry.</li>
    <li><strong><code>api_error</code>:</strong> An unexpected API error occurred. The skill surfaces the message verbatim — check the Auth0 status page if it persists.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="where-this-fits">Where this fits</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Pillar:</strong> <a href={join('/how-it-works/')}>Skills management</a></li>
    <li><strong>Decision:</strong> <a href="https://github.com/shannoncarver/hackathon-may-2026/blob/main/docs/decisions/0020-auth0-sec-skill.md">0020 — Auth0 sec skill</a> (see also <a href="https://github.com/shannoncarver/hackathon-may-2026/blob/main/docs/decisions/0014-auth0-logs-skill.md">0014</a> for the sibling hybrid-approach decision)</li>
    <li><strong>Source:</strong> <a href="https://github.com/shannoncarver/hackathon-may-2026/blob/main/.claude/skills/auth0-sec/SKILL.md"><code>.claude/skills/auth0-sec/SKILL.md</code></a></li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="next">Try the next skill</h2>
  <p>After confirming security posture, move to <a href={join('/tutorials/ha-debug/')}>ha-debug</a> for full per-user state inspection—account metadata, connection details, and recent session history.</p>
</ArticleLayout>
